          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "دست آخر (dast-e âkhar) — \"at the very end, in the end (colloquial)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "دست آخر (dast-e âkhar) همه با هم شام خوردیم. Dast-e âkhar hame bâ ham shâm khordim. In the end we all had dinner together."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Conversational. Literally \"the last hand\", as in a card game. Written without the ezafe sign, read dast-e."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dast \"hand\" is inherited, from Old Persian dasta- (EtymDB); âkhar \"last\" is borrowed from Arabic ākhir (EtymDB). The phrase is the last hand of a game."
            }
          ]
        },
